# gcc — real-world examples

## Compile a file (produces a.out)

```sh
gcc file.c
```

## Compile with a custom output name

```sh
gcc -o file file.c
```

## Compile with debug symbols for gdb

```sh
gcc -g -o app main.c
gcc -ggdb3 -o app main.c    # richest gdb debug info
```

## Force a 64-bit build

```sh
gcc -m64 -o app main.c
```

## Add a system include path (headers there raise no warnings)

```sh
gcc -isystem /usr/include/myPersonnal/lib/ -o app main.c
```

## Windows GUI build under MinGW (no console window)

```sh
gcc -mwindows -o app.exe main.c
```

## The everyday homelab compile: warnings on, optimized

```sh
gcc -Wall -Wextra -O2 -o sensor-poll sensor-poll.c
```

## Link the math library

```sh
gcc -o calc calc.c -lm
```

## Compile to object files, then link (multi-file project)

```sh
gcc -c util.c && gcc -c main.c
gcc -o app main.o util.o
```

| Flag | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| -o FILE | name the output |
| -c | compile to .o, don't link |
| -g / -ggdb3 | debug symbols |
| -Wall -Wextra | enable most warnings |
| -O2 | optimize |
| -lNAME | link library libNAME |
| -I DIR | add include search path |
| -m64 | 64-bit target |
